'''''Aizoon pubescens''''' ('''Galenia''' or '''Coastal Galenia''') is a low-growing perennial herb in the family [[Aizoaceae]].<ref name=NSW/><ref name=WA>{{FloraBase|name=''Galenia pubescens'' |id=2801}}</ref>  It is native to [[South Africa]] ([[Cape Provinces]], [[Free State (province)|Free State]], and [[Northern Provinces]]) and naturalised elsewhere.<ref name = powo/><ref name=NSW>{{cite web |url=http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Galenia~pubescens |title=''Galenia pubescens'' |accessdate=2009-04-25 |author=|work= PlantNET - New South Wales Flora Online |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens & Domain Trust, Sydney Australia}}</ref>

==Description==
The species is [[prostrate shrub|prostrate]] or [[decumbent]], with [[ovate (leaf)|ovate]] to [[spatulate]] leaves which are covered with hairs when young.<ref name=NSW/> The flowers are white with a slight pink tinge, and yellow with age.<ref name=NSW/> They are followed by capsules which contain shiny, black seeds, about 1&nbsp;mm in length.<ref name=NSW/>

==Naturalisation==
In Australia, the species is naturalised in [[Western Australia]], [[South Australia]], the [[Northern Territory]], [[Tasmania]], [[Victoria (Australia)|Victoria]] and [[New South Wales]].<ref name=APNI>{{cite web |url=http://www.anbg.gov.au/cgi-bin/apni?TAXON_NAME=GALENIA+PUBESCENS|title=''Galenia pubscens'' |accessdate=2009-04-25 |work= [[Australian Plant Name Index]] (APNI), IBIS database|publisher = Centre for Plant Biodiversity Research, Australian Government, Canberra}}</ref> In [[New South Wales]], under the Noxious Weeds Act 1993, it is regarded as a noxious weed in the [[Liverpool Plains]] and [[Tamworth, New South Wales|Tamworth]] regions.<ref name=NSW/>

The species is disliked by some beekeepers. Although it produces nectar profusely and bees like it, the nectar makes honey taste slightly bitter.{{Citation needed|date=June 2010}}

